WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.589 --> 00:00:02.371
- [Host] Let's talk about football.

00:00:02.371 --> 00:00:03.607
- [Co-Host] Blue, 42.

00:00:03.607 --> 00:00:05.059
This football, right?

00:00:05.059 --> 00:00:08.328
- [Host] No, today, we're
talking about this football.

00:00:08.328 --> 00:00:09.277
- [Co-Host] Soccer?

00:00:09.277 --> 00:00:11.179
- [Host] No, it's football.

00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:14.928
You have the players, the
managers, and the tea.

00:00:14.928 --> 00:00:15.808
- [Co-Host] The tea?

00:00:15.808 --> 00:00:17.669
- [Host] Mr. Parish, please explain.

00:00:17.669 --> 00:00:20.080
- Welcome to you all, to
Nottingham Forest F.C.,

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.400
and have a great time while you're here.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.138
And don't forget, if you can,
try a cup of my Tom's Tea.

00:00:26.138 --> 00:00:29.055
(laughs) The best.

00:00:31.739 --> 00:00:33.374
- [Host] Nottingham Forest is one

00:00:33.374 --> 00:00:35.771
of the oldest football clubs in the world.

00:00:35.771 --> 00:00:38.360
They had a great run about 40 years ago,

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.309
winning two European cups
and loads of other stuff.

00:00:41.309 --> 00:00:43.120
- No, I'm very proud of this team.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.037
Happy days.

00:00:44.904 --> 00:00:46.360
- [Host] But whilst those days are gone,

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.847
there's still one world-class
tradition that remains.

00:00:49.847 --> 00:00:51.207
- Well, my name is Tom Parish.

00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:55.319
I'm almost 89 now, not
long before the 90th.

00:00:55.319 --> 00:00:58.259
I'm a tea boy, and I love it.

00:00:58.259 --> 00:00:59.888
People will say, "A tea boy?"

00:00:59.888 --> 00:01:02.509
I said, "Yeah, I worked
my way up." (chuckles)

00:01:02.509 --> 00:01:04.558
- [Host] Here in Britain,
it was a common occurrence

00:01:04.558 --> 00:01:08.200
for football clubs to have
a tea lady or a tea boy.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.469
Sadly, these traditions
are on the decline.

00:01:11.469 --> 00:01:13.153
But, what does a tea boy do?

00:01:13.153 --> 00:01:15.214
- Oh, crikey, what does the tea boy do?

00:01:15.214 --> 00:01:16.614
He makes tea, of course.

00:01:16.614 --> 00:01:19.332
- [Host] So Tom isn't handing
out tea at the sideline.

00:01:19.332 --> 00:01:22.742
He has his own kitchen next
to the teams' dressing rooms.

00:01:22.742 --> 00:01:24.795
- Let me show you around.

00:01:24.795 --> 00:01:26.942
- [Host] His job is to
make classic cups of tea

00:01:26.942 --> 00:01:30.593
for managers, players,
directors, photographers.

00:01:30.593 --> 00:01:31.980
- That was one of our photographers.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.084
Yes, he's a good lad.

00:01:34.084 --> 00:01:35.172
- [Host] The list goes on.

00:01:35.172 --> 00:01:38.392
So Tom, what are your
top tips for making tea?

00:01:38.392 --> 00:01:42.559
- You get the best tea,
and that is Yorkshire Tea.

00:01:43.564 --> 00:01:46.492
Boiling water over the tea bag.

00:01:46.492 --> 00:01:48.102
Get it to the sides of the cup

00:01:48.102 --> 00:01:49.902
and squeeze it as much as you can

00:01:49.902 --> 00:01:52.164
to get the juice out of the tea bag.

00:01:52.164 --> 00:01:53.873
Put your milk in.

00:01:53.873 --> 00:01:57.373
That should be, I hope, a good cup of tea.

00:01:58.673 --> 00:02:00.923
(cheering)

00:02:04.702 --> 00:02:06.084
- It's not just a football match.

00:02:06.084 --> 00:02:08.052
It's something that you look forward to,

00:02:08.052 --> 00:02:10.755
and it's there every week.

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:12.761
That's what keeps you going.

00:02:12.761 --> 00:02:14.344
And, I love it, love it.

00:02:15.222 --> 00:02:18.702
As long as I can walk, and
that the club will have me,

00:02:18.702 --> 00:02:21.452
I'll be here making a cup of tea.

